   100	## Thornwater, days 54–58
   101	
   102	- **The ladder inventory:** the crew sleeps in the loft from the 54th, because the boxes are there. Each morning Cael takes stock at the ladder's head, as the log's inventory is taken at the start of anything: who can get down, and how.
   103	- **The barn-table library:** three boxes on their sides on the sack-sewing trestle, lids off, mouths outward: shelves with no wall. Karis's line: "everything I know about the wrong country". Lira is made scribe; Cael turns pages. The finding is copied into the field archive from his reading.
   104	- **The cart-yard, day 58 (Karis's window, ≈1,400).** Three east carts come for grain. Lira asks a carter with a clouded eye where his road goes; he does not talk roads to paper. Karis is dictating notes on the carter's map to Cael: east of the gate, two days of ticks out, the double line leaves its straight, goes round bare hide and comes back; she calls it a quiet place on the map. The carter's wife, passing with a sack, a small boy on her cart's foot-board, says the protected three sentences and drives out. Four questions arrive and Karis keeps all four: that is the cost of learning the other way. She has Cael write the three sentences, read them back twice, and underline *everybody knows* twice. "Is it a source?" / "Not a good one. The first." The leaf goes into the middle box. (The bend on the hide is mine; it agrees with the Fallow slip in BOOK_MAP §10. Flagged.)
   105	
   106	## The road down, days 58–59
   107	
   108	- **The steading's woman's last word.** Lira, from the cart's tail, asks where anybody out here keeps the numbers: rank, who is above whom. "They stop inside, where the tiers are". The bar goes across. (I read "they" as the ranks. Flagged.)
   109	- **Lira on the cart's tail**, announcing that it was her decision.
   110	- The ford in daylight: nothing on the bar; the lip stones reset, white, all in their row; two open.
   111	- **Camp above the wash, the 59th.** The first thing the finding buys is a fire: a flint is not a declaration. Seln strikes it. The carter, twenty-two years on a cold road, sits down at it in the end. (Flagged.)
   112	- **Lira at the fire:** Norhold told her to a tenth how far she was from Silver; a ladder was built to her height and she walked off it at full price; she ran the best thirty paces of her life at the ford and nothing says so. "So what am I becoming, exactly, if nobody's measuring?" Nobody answers. Cael does not either: the fact that set him free is the fact that starves her. He writes the question down and writes no answer under it.
   113	
   114	## Lowmarch, day 60
   115	
   116	- **Fourteen slips** again: the dry roads have taken crews and paper out.
   117	- **Seln** reads the plank from post to post in the open and takes down the well's slip, which has hung since the ninth day. "I keep waiting for someone to ask for my report." Cael does not ask: the man's want is not to be asked.
   118	- **Brom at the plank's end.** "What does the board pay a man with no Path at all." / "Same as anyone," … "What'd he do?" Brom says what he did. He writes Pike's answer on the back of a dead slip and puts it inside his shirt: the second thing anybody ever said to him that he meant to keep (the first was Rooke's four words at a tailboard, and he only had to remember those). Pike, after him: "Held a dog with a shoulder and a river, that one."
   119	- **The mail** sorted aloud at the plank, each outside said twice. One is addressed only with his name and "past the Line". **Hesk's letter** (protected). The grandmother: Hesk has never used the word to him; he does not know whose mother she was, and does not guess. No roof yet; no answer yet.
   120	- **The saying.** Forty marks. Teague came in on the 57th, read the struck slip and the line, took two holds' slips and went out at next light without a word. Pike rewrites the sheet by hand: **twelfth**. "Teague's still eleven up. He'll speak to you when you've cost him something. That's not rudeness. That's the ledger."
   121	
   122	## The well, days 61–64 (new on the page)
   123	
   124	- The slip has hung fifty-one days. Pike says why when he marks it: it went sour at the thaw; a carter's boy went down for a dropped bucket and was drawn up asleep. It is the well at the south road's fork, half a day out on flat ground, paid from the standing-money because it is everybody's well.
   125	- **Seln walks it first** on the 61st and says nothing. At the curb on the 62nd there is fresh soot a fathom down and a candle stub on a knotted string under a stone. Cael reads it and lets him keep it.
   126	- **The hazard is bad air.** Karis has one page that is right in both countries: foul air lies in a shaft like water in a cup; a flame shows its surface; it is bailed or stirred out. The candle dies at fifteen feet. Brom binds a faggot of thorn; Lira and Cael wind it up and down the shaft. Brom, forbidden to pull, calls the knots.
   127	- **Cael goes down**, because the one whole pair of legs is wanted at the windlass. A candle hangs six feet under his boots; Karis counts aloud and he answers each ten. The fragments have nothing to say to air. He loses a count before the candle shows anything, and calls "Up" before he is sure. The candle is out at twenty feet when they lower it again. Shorter turns on the 63rd. The fallen course reset; the shaft drawn until it runs clean; Brom tastes it. A southbound carter drinks and scratches a wheel on the slip.
   128	
