   159	
   160	## Withheld (checked against the packet)
   161	
   162	Deployment: none through day 52, one from the dusk of day 53, and one thereafter. Shadow unused. Nobody learns the mechanism. Quiet ground stays three sentences from a carter's wife; nobody asks Pike or anyone else. No healer, no Tide practitioner; the ankle is not mended. Teague is absent; no meeting. Karis's hypothesis is a finding only after the fight, and is entered in Cael's hand at her dictation. No new name for any person or animal. No season, month or weekday names; no year count since the Kindling; Cael is eighteen; never "his Path" of Cael. No stated stride length for Karis. No 110-yard answer.
   163	
   164	## Amendments made while drafting (recorded after the prose; the list above is as first written)
   165	
   166	- **The stone count** on the far shingle is seventeen at noon on day 52 and twenty at dusk on day 53 (the list had nineteen and twenty-two). The word for the number after eighteen appears nowhere in the seven chapters.
   167	- **Who carries the slip:** Cael takes it up at the plank and carries it inside his shirt; the steading's woman marks it on a barrel-head in the barn.
   168	- **Karis's stride is never counted.** At the ford she has Cael pace for her ("I want it in yards, and mine aren't"); thirty by eight is in his yards.
   169	- **The count is spoken down** through the fight as the listener's handhold: "Six" (Lira), "Five" (Brom), "Four left" (Lira), "Three" (Cael), then "Five" dead (Lira) and "two that I can't call" (Karis). Lira's hound is taken a few breaths before Brom's stops moving, but she speaks her number after his.
   170	- **Brom's hold:** the hound's head and forelegs go under in the fast shallow water beside his slab, pinned by his shoulder; the current swings its hindquarters over the lip, so that the river pulls one way and his heel against the stone's rim holds the other. The forearm is opened by the jaws dragging as the river hauls.
   171	- **Karis's knife** is the small horn-handled knife she sharpens pencils with. The hound strikes her knife hand; she catches the knife up by the blade and braces the handle against her breastbone. Two cuts across each palm: the first hold and the second.
   172	- **The carter sets one white stone** in the row before he loads the cart: the stone that had been left lying out of its line on the forty-ninth.
   173	- **The survivor comes to the barn door once**, on the fourth night ("Left." / "Right." / "Between us we've the pair").
   174	- **The bowman cuts Lira's stick** on the 56th (the third day of the stay) and says nothing with it.
   175	- **The boy** who rode down with the news is the boy who fetched the hammer for the broken rung in chapter 6. Pike keeps him until the mule is fit; he comes back up with the carter on the 57th.
   176	- **The day-51 purchase** is "a mark's worth of flour and bacon"; in hand, nine. The hold takes one mark for linen and thread on the 58th.
   177	- **Lira's distances:** she is drawn "twenty yards down the gravel, or more"; her run is "twenty-five yards"; at the fire she calls it thirty.
   178	- **At the plank on day 51** Lira names three of the five by tier to Pike ("an Iron, Rank One, … an Iron, Rank Three, and a Copper. And two that haven't said."); Pike has no column for it. Seln's tier and Cael's nothing stay unsaid in public.
   179	- **The wall on day 60:** the line that drops to thirteenth is entered as *the shingle pair* (a trade, not a name).
   180	- **The well:** the sign of Seln's first walk is a fresh spot of candle-grease on the curb and a candle stub on a knotted string under a stone. The southbound carter who witnesses the slip is the father of the boy who was drawn up asleep.
   181	- **Brom on Iron Skin over the wound** (day 66): "On a road that's a declaration. In a meadow it's a lie. It mends as an arm, or it doesn't."
   182	- **The closing beat** is Pike setting a bare tack by the left-hand post for a hold's slip he has had word of "from a carter off the north-west": "It's got a ford." Nothing else is said of it.
   183	- **Windows as drafted:** Lira about 1,910 words in two scenes (chapter 10); Karis about 1,520 in one (chapter 12).
   184	- **Rhythm:** the first drafts of chapters 9 and 11–14 measured short (means 10.6–11.5; under 2% of sentences at forty words). Each was re-composed by reading in the same session: related statements joined in narration and in the longer speeches of Seln, Karis and Pike, and a few over-long joins parted again. System vocabulary was then made plainer in the fight and its aftermath (declaration, discharge, Path, the fragment names) where figures of speech had been standing in for it.

