   113	"There's no black on them."
   114	
   115	"It's rained," said Lira.
   116	
   117	"It's rained on the dung as well, and that's still here." He stood up. "And there's no ash under the grass, for I've had my hand in. Nobody's lit a fire in that. Not once."
   118	
   119	"Then what's a ring of stones for?"
   120	
   121	Brom thought about it honestly. He had no answer, and he said so, and went to see to the horse.
   122	
   123	So they laid their own fire in it. Seln's flint was at the bottom of his coat. Karis put out one finger and lit the kindling with a point no bigger than a pin's head, as she had lit a page for Cael under a cart two nights before. The red wood caught fast and burned with a high clear flame; and its smoke stood up out of the valley, straight, in the still air, to twice the height of the trees, where anything with eyes on any of seven ridges might have looked at it.
   124	
   125	It was a good fire. They cooked on it and sat round it late, and nobody had told them anything yet.
   126	
   127	---
   128	
   129	On the fourth day the river they were following met another, and the road went into the water.
   130	
   131	It was a wide place, a hundred feet across and more, where the two streams ran together over shingle, and from the bank it looked no deeper than a boot. A cairn stood on the near side, a neat one, waist high, a good forty yards upstream of where the ruts went in; and there was another on the far bank, as far downstream.
   132	
   133	"Straight over," said Lira, who had the gelding's cheek-strap. "It's nothing. It's the upland one again."
   134	
   135	"Then why has somebody built two cairns?" said Karis.
   136	
   137	"To show where the ford is."
   138	
   139	"They aren't opposite each other."
   140	
   141	Lira looked, and she had her mouth open to answer when a mule came down through the red trees on the far side, with a rider on it, and walked into the river at the cairn.
   142	
   143	The rider did not hail them. She put the mule's nose at the upstream cairn on their own bank and came across on the slant, a long quartering line, and the water was never over the animal's knee. Halfway she passed within ten yards of the place where Lira had meant to cross, and Cael watched the surface there as she went by: it was smooth, and darker than the rest, and it turned slowly in its own length, like something asleep.
   144	
   145	"Oh," said Lira.
   146	
   147	"How deep?" said Brom.
   148	
   149	"I don't know. Over the bed of the cart." She let go of the strap and wiped her hand down her coat. "I'd have put three boxes in that."
   150	
   151	The mule came up out of the shallows beside their cairn and stood dripping. The woman on it was fifty or so, brown and spare, with a lip that had been split a long time ago and had healed a little crooked. Her hat had the brim cut away on the right side. A hatchet hung in a loop at her saddle-bow, edge outward, where anybody could see it, and behind her lay two flat leather bags with long flaps.
   152	
   153	She looked them over. She took in the horse's feet, and the iron on the wheels, and the shape of the canvas, and gave each about as long as a clerk gives a line he has seen before. Then she lifted her chin at the cairn beside her and at the one across the water, once each, and turned the mule, and rode it forty yards down the bank before she let it drink.
   154	
   155	Cael understood the second thing a moment after the first. An animal drinking stirs up the bed, and a ford is a thing people read by its colour; so one watered below the line, and left the crossing clean for whoever came next. Nobody had told her that he needed it explained, and she had not explained it.
   156	
   157	"She is going west," said Seln quietly. "I think she will be the only one who is."
   158	
   159	Cael had the two letters inside his coat. He had carried them two days in the pocket of Hesk's book, where a Gold's letter had lain for most of a year, and he had not expected to be rid of them before a town. He walked down the bank to her.
   160	
   161	"Will you carry two?"
   162	
   163	She put out her hand. He gave them to her, and she did not turn them over to read the outsides; she laid them on her palm, one and then the other, and weighed them.
   164	
   165	"How far past the turf fire?"
   166	
   167	"A long way. One's for Denvash, and the other's Ardenmere."
   168	
   169	"Far end, the both." She thought. "A copper each to the fire. What the old man asks to send them down his hill is his and not mine, and they'll want it at the other end."
   170	
   171	"That's fair."
   172	
   173	"It's the price." She opened the nearer bag and slid them in among the rest and buckled the flap.
   174	
   175	He waited. A ford-keeper had asked him for his name not a fortnight ago, and had been a little sorry not to get it, and a man on a rock had wanted to know where she was to write to. So he stood on the shingle with two coppers in his hand, and waited to be asked what the letters were, and who he was, and where five people thought they were going with a shod horse and three roped boxes.
   176	
   177	She took the coppers and looked up the valley, over his head.
   178	
   179	"You've had fires," she said. "I saw the one last night from the top. And the one before."
   180	
   181	"Yes."
   182	
   183	"Hm."
   184	
   185	"Is that wrong?" said Lira, from the cart.
   186	
   187	The woman looked at her without unkindness, as one looks at a child who has asked a guest how much he earns.
   188	
   189	"It's your wood," she said.
   190	
   191	She gathered the mule's head and rode up through the red trees by the way they had come down, and she did not look round. Nobody out here seemed to, and he had begun to think that it was a kind of manners.
   192	
   193	They took the cart over on the slanting line, cairn to cairn, and the water never reached the hub. On the far bank Lira walked the gelding forty yards downstream before she let him put his head down, and she did it without a word to anybody, with her chin up.
   194	
   195	Cael sat on the shingle and wrote while the horse drank.
   196	
   197	*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
   198	
   199	He looked at the last line a while. Then he added the other, which was getting to be a habit.
   200	
   201	*Deployment since the Line: none.*
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	Below the ford it began to be a road.
   206	
   207	Other ruts came into theirs out of side valleys, one after another, like streams joining; and by the fifth day's noon the track was a cart wide and beaten hard, with the grass gone from its middle. The valley had opened. The red trees stood back on the slopes, and there was pale grass in the bottom, shoulder high, that went over in the wind like a cat under a hand.
   208	
   209	There were deer in it, of a sort. They were grey and long in the leg, with white rumps, and they stood in the shallows by threes and watched the cart go by with the steady disapproval of magistrates. Brom watched them back.
   210	
   211	"They're not afraid of us."
   212	
   213	"Should they be?" said Lira.
   214	
   215	"Deer are afraid of people. Every deer I ever met was." He walked a few paces. "Either nobody hunts these, or whatever hunts them doesn't look like us."
   216	
   217	"What doesn't look like us?"
   218	
   219	"I'm not saying anything's so. I'm only saying they stand very easy, for an animal you can eat."
   220	
   221	The gelding's shoes rang on the stones. It had not mattered on the upland, where there was turf, and Cael had not heard it in the ruts. But the road was hard now, and wherever rock came through it the iron struck, and the valley gave the sound back. He caught Seln listening to it, with his head a little on one side.
   222	
   223	"He can be heard from that ridge," said Seln.
   224	
   225	"He's a horse," said Lira. "He's got feet."
   226	
   227	"So has a mule, and a mule goes unshod on this ground. I have been looking at the prints for two days, as the large one has, and I do not think that anybody on this road has ever wished to be heard from a ridge." He considered the gelding without ill will. "I do not say he is wrong. I say he is an announcement."
   228	
   229	"He hasn't got anything to announce."
   230	
   231	"He is announcing us."
   232	
   233	Lira opened her mouth, and shut it, and walked on with her hand a little tighter on the strap.
   234	
   235	They went on late, since the light held. Cael walked last, behind the tailboard, where the dust was; he had taken that place because his arm was sound again and he thought it his turn, and because from the back he could see all four of them and the road behind as well. He had not stopped looking at the road behind; he had only stopped expecting anything of it.
   236	
   237	The sun went off the valley floor. It did so all at once out here, when the western ridge took it, and the grass turned from gold to grey between one breath and the next.
   238	
   239	It lifted.
   240	
   241	It was Shadow, the one fragment of the nine that he kept sealed. He knew it at once, and he was ready, as he had been ready at every dusk since a door log in a wool exchange. It came up soft behind the other eight and offered him what it always offered, which was to make less of him. He told it no. He had told it no so many times that the word had worn smooth, and he was tired, with eleven miles in his legs, and he said it as a man says good night.
   242	
   243	His boots stopped making any sound.
   244	
   245	He did not notice for a stride, and then the gravel was not answering him. He put his left foot down on loose stones and they took it in silence, like wool, and his right the same; and he looked down at his own feet walking a hard road with no more noise than a cat makes on a stair, and for one stride more he let it be, out of pure astonishment.
   246	
   247	Then he shut it, as he would have shut a lid, with his whole hand. The stones spoke again under his heel, loudly and at once, and he had never been so glad of a small ugly sound.
   248	
   249	"Your left boot went first," said Seln.
   250	
   251	He was at the off wheel, ten paces ahead, and he had not turned round.
   252	
   253	"I've got it."
   254	
   255	"You have it now. For four strides you had not, and by the second it was both feet." Seln walked on a little. When he spoke again it was no louder, and the others, at the horse's head, could not have heard. "I should say that I know it. I learned it on a stair, as a boy, by wanting very much not to be heard by the person at the top. It took me a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident. It always came at dusk, when the eye is worst and the ear must do the work."
   256	
   257	"I didn't ask it for anything."
   258	
   259	"No. It is not a thing that waits to be asked, at that hour, by a tired man, and I was a tired man for some years." He turned his head a very little, not so far as Cael. "It was not spent. Nobody was on the road to see it, and I have the best reason of anyone living to know what it sounds like. I only remark that I heard a person stop being there, and I should prefer that you did not grow to like it."
   260	
   261	"I didn't like it."
   262	
   263	"Good. I did, and that was the difficulty."
   264	
   265	Seln went forward to the wheel and laid a hand on the tailboard, as though it had needed steadying, which it had not.
   266	
   267	Cael wrote it that night by the fire, in pencil, with the book on his knee.
   268	
   269	*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
   270	
   271	---
   272	
   273	They came up with the carts in the middle of the sixth morning.
   274	
   275	There were three, going the same way, at a mule's walk. They were narrow things, high in the wheel, hardly more than half the width of the road, and Cael saw why when the first of them went by a place where the bank had slipped: it passed with a foot to spare, and their own cart, coming after, had to be lifted round.
   276	
   277	"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
   278	
   279	"How long?"
   280	
   281	"She never said. I think that's rather the point."
   282	
   283	So they fell in, forty yards back. A woman walked beside the last cart, which was stacked with hides under a net, and she looked round once, at the gelding's feet, with a look that Cael had begun to know. Then she faced front again, and that was all the greeting there was.
   284	
   285	He watched them for the whole of a day, having nothing else to do with his eyes.
   286	
   287	They did not talk. He noticed that first, and it was a good hour before he believed it. An old man led, with two mules, and behind him came a boy of fifteen or so with one, and then the woman; and when the old man wanted the boy he did not call back to him, but lifted a hand, and the boy looked. Once the woman clicked her tongue, twice, and the old man slowed. From the middle of the morning until the sun stood in the west Cael heard no word spoken above the creak of the wheels.
   288	
   289	They wore steel, and they wore it outside. The old man had a hatchet through his belt, over his coat, with the edge forward, and the woman carried a long knife on her thigh and had a boar spear slung in rings along the cart's side, where one pull would free it. Even the boy had a hedging bill, too big for him. None of it was hidden and none of it was held; it was simply there to be seen, like the courier's hatchet in its loop.
   290	
   291	At noon they stopped and ate, and Lira went a few steps up the bank, by habit, for sticks.
   292	
   293	"Don't," said Brom.
   294	
   295	"We've flour. I was going to—"
   296	
   297	"They're not." He jerked his head up the road. "Look at them."
   298	
   299	The carters sat on the shafts of their own carts, each with a cloth open on the knee, eating something cold and flat, and none of them had so much as glanced at the wood lying under the trees. Lira came back down the bank with her hands empty.
   300	
   301	They halted at dusk, where the road widened by the river. The old man pulled his cart off the crown and the boy pulled in behind him. Then each of them walked to a ring of stones in the trodden ground, and took up one in either hand, and set it behind a wheel.
   302	
   303	"Chocks," said Brom, under his breath, like a man who has found his own boot. "It's where they keep the chocks."
   304	
   305	It was cold by the water once the sun had gone, and nobody lit anything.
   306	
   307	The boy came back along the carts while there was still some light. He had been looking at the gelding over his shoulder all day and wanted a nearer look. He grinned at Lira as boys had been grinning at her since Ardenmere, and stood there with his thumbs in his belt. Then he seemed to feel that five strangers and a horse deserved something of him. He took out his right hand and turned it palm up, with the fingers spread, as Karis spread hers before she set a point.
   308	
   309	A hand came down across his wrist.
   310	
   311	It was not a blow. The old man had come up behind him without a sound, and he put the boy's arm down as one puts down the lid of a box, and the boy's ears went red.
   312	
   313	"Not on the road," said the old man.
   314	
   315	He did not raise his voice, and in that quiet he had no need to. The boy went away along the carts with his head down.
   316	
   317	The old man stayed where he was. He looked at the iron tyres, and the three shapes under the canvas, and the shod horse, and at each of the five of them in turn, and he was a good while about it. He had pale eyes, and the skin round them was folded very small.
   318	
   319	"You're paper, aren't you," he said.
   320	
   321	Cael had never heard the word used so, and he knew what it meant before he had finished hearing it. It meant a person from the country of stamps and seals, who had been written down.
   322	
   323	"Yes," he said.
   324	
   325	"Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it."
   326	
   327	He went back to his mules. He had said nothing further, and even so it came to more than the woman on the mule had given them.
   328	
   329	Cael sat down on the cart's shaft. He did what he had done on the east tier of a yard every week for two years, with a notebook on his knee, and it steadied him as it always had: he set out the claim, and what stood under it, and then he ruled.
   330	
   331	The claim was that out here nobody on a road declared a Path, at any tier, for any purpose; or lit a fire he could do without, or spoke where a hand would serve; and that everybody showed steel.
   332	
   333	Under it he had five things. Three carts had gone a day without a word, and every one of their people wore an edge outside the coat, the boy included. There were rings of stones on the halting-grounds that had never been burned in, because they had never been meant for fire. A courier had counted their smokes from a ridge and told them it was their wood. And a boy had been about to show them something, and had had it taken out of his hand.
   334	
   335	He did not know why, and that was the weakness in the ruling, which he looked at squarely, for not one of the five things said what the rule was a rule against. But he knew what a rule looked like when a whole country kept it and nobody troubled to say it aloud.
   336	
   337	"Hold," he said.
   338	
   339	They were all near enough to hear. Lira had her chin on her fist.
   340	
   341	"Hold what?"
   342	
   343	"All of it. Whatever each of you has. Nobody declares anything on a road, for any reason, until we know what it brings." He kept his voice where the old man had kept his. "Not a Wind burst. Not Iron Skin. Not an Ember point to light a pipe by."
   344	
   345	"I haven't done one thing since the Line," said Lira.
   346	
   347	"Karis has lit three fires with her finger."
   348	
   349	"Four," said Karis. "There was the one under the cart. I've entered all of them, with the hour." She had gone a little paler. "I shall enter that I didn't know."
   350	
   351	"And if a wheel goes down in a river?" said Brom.
   352	
   353	"Then you lift it like a large man."
   354	
   355	"I am a large man."
   356	
   357	"Then it ought to go well."
   358	
   359	Brom thought that over, and seemed to find it sound.
   360	
   361	"And you?" said Lira, looking at him. "You've nine fragments, and not one of them's a Path. Does it count?"
   362	
   363	"I don't know that either. So I hold all nine." It sounded like more, said aloud. "It's the one thing I've had real practice at."
   364	
   365	He wrote the day's lines in what light there was. *Sixth day. Deployment since the Line: none. Ruling: hold.* Then he put the book away and ate cold flour-cake with the rest of them, in the dark, with the river going by.
   366	
   367	Forty yards up the road, somebody else's mule shifted its feet. Nobody anywhere lit a thing.

