    49	Cael did what he always did, and began with the claim, which was a mile of road that carters crossed only at one hour of the day. Under it stood two carts that had not come home, and a price that the whole meadow thought too small, and a word, *quiet*, that everybody out here used for a thing done and also for a thing that happened to a cart. He had no notion what walked on that stretch; but a man who had kept the road for twenty-two years had just said what he wanted for it, and it was the one thing they had spent a month learning to do.
    50	
    51	"Hands," he said, low.
    52	
    53	Brom's went up first, and then Seln's. Lira's followed, with her jaw set, for she had not forgiven the answer. Karis kept hers down.
    54	
    55	"The boxes," she said. "I left them three days in that loft, for a fence, and I didn't sleep. This is two days out and I can't tell how many back."
    56	
    57	The carter had heard her. Out here, it seemed, everybody heard everything.
    58	
    59	"What boxes?"
    60	
    61	"Three. They're paper."
    62	
