   216	
   217	They walked every foot of the place, and it took them most of the morning.
   218	
   219	The rock was worse than the carter had said. The green on it was a short close weed, and the water had combed it all one way, and a boot set down on it had no more hold than on a fish. Brom went along the lip on the near side with the bill reversed in his hand, feeling ahead of him with the ash haft as a blind man feels with a stick. Where the white stones stopped he reached out past the edge and let the haft go down. It went in to his fist, and his arm after it to the shoulder, and he drew it up again with nothing on the end.
   220	
   221	"It doesn't shelve," he said. "It stops. There's a wall of rock under that lip, straight down, and I can't find the floor."
   222	
   223	Karis dropped a pebble where he had tried, and watched it sink through the green water, turning, until it was a pale speck and then nothing.
   224	
