   264	
   265	"It rolls," she said, walking back up with her hands on her hips. "The spine's firm. You can run on the spine as if it were a road. But the last five yards are round stones lying on round stones, with the water through them, and the edges are worse. If I had to stop there in a hurry I'd want to do it short."
   266	
   267	"Then stop short."
   268	
   269	"I'm telling you so that I'll remember I said it."
   270	
   271	Seln had risen from the tail. He came up the bar to them with his coat let down, and there was wet gravel on one knee of his breeches and nothing whatever in his face.
   272	
   273	"I should like two hours," he said, "and I should like them alone. I am going to lie in the grass on that bank and look at this river with nobody in it. Go back up the road to the first bend, and lie down in the scrub, and be dull. If any one of you has a Path that wishes to be let out, this is the place to refuse it."
   274	
   275	"Two hours of what?" said Lira.
   276	
   277	"Of nothing. It is the part of my trade that nobody admires."
   278	
   279	So they went, and it was the hardest hold that Cael had kept since the stretch.
   280	
   281	There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
   282	
   283	Lira was asleep within four breaths. Brom lay with his hands behind his head and his lips moving, and Cael was fairly sure that he was going over the virtues of a rock. Karis wrote for the whole of the two hours, with Hesk's clock propped against her knee, and never once looked up the road after a cart.
   284	
   285	Seln came back when the shadows had only begun to lean.
   286	
   287	He was dry. There was grass seed on his shoulders and down one sleeve, and he sat among them and held out his hand for the water-skin. He drank a good deal of it before he spoke.
   288	
   289	"Seven," he said.
   290	
   291	They waited.
   292	
   293	"Is that all you're going to say?" said Lira.
   294	
   295	"It is all that will keep until there is a roof. The rest wants a floor to draw on, and I should like to draw it once." He handed back the skin and stood. "We have a mile of grass to walk at an hour that no carter would choose. I suggest that we begin."
   296	
   297	"Seven what?" said Karis. She had her pencil ready, and her voice was not altogether steady.
   298	
   299	Seln looked down at her. It seemed to Cael that he was deciding how much a person was owed who had waited a fortnight with one leaf.
   300	
   301	"Seven that came down to drink," he said. "One at a time. And not one of the seven would wet a foot to do it."

