     1	# Chapter 9 — It's Because You Die
     2	
     3	They walked the stretch with the sun two hands above the ridge behind them, and nothing came out of the grass.
     4	
     5	Cael had not expected that to be hard, and it was the worst mile of the four he had made on that ground. There was no cart this time, and no buckle; there were five people in two ruts, with Seln thirty paces out on the left verge and Brom at the tail, and the pale grass stood chest-high on either hand with the light coming through it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other. Lira went the whole way with her right hand half lifted and her head a little on one side, listening for a hole in that noise, and her hand never rose above her belt.
     6	
     7	He put nine things down at every stride, all the same, since they had no means of knowing that it was the wrong hour.
     8	
