    16	
    17	"I sent for the one at the top of his sheet," she said.
    18	
    19	"He's a day and a half up the cold road," said Cael. "Pike struck his hold. In front of the meadow."
    20	
    21	"Struck it."
    22	
    23	"With one line. We took the slip."
    24	
    25	She went over them slowly, and Cael knew what she was reading, for he had read it himself in a jug's worth of lamplight three nights ago: four lines in a book, and nothing gone wrong in any of them.
    26	
    27	"And he's sent me the bottom."
    28	
    29	"Yes."
    30	
    31	"Hm."
    32	
    33	It was the sound she had made over a lantern when he told her that he was paper; he had thought then that she was putting the answer by for later, and it seemed that later had come.
    34	
