    46	
    47	Cael counted fourteen, from an old man asleep in the chimney corner to a child on somebody's knee. They sat down both sides of a trestle with bowls in front of them, and when five strangers came in under the lintel the talk dropped and did not rise again. A place had been left at the hearth end of the board; and on the settle beyond it sat a man with his right arm laid along a folded sack.
    48	
    49	He was thirty-five or so, thin and brown, with three days of stubble on him. The arm was bound in clean linen from the tips of the fingers to the elbow, so thickly that it had no shape, and one of his legs was thrust out stiff before him. He did not shift it when they sat.
    50	
