     5	He tried it across the yard, from the barn door to the well. He went out limping a little, with the linen tight round his calf, and counted from habit; and at the twelfth stride the whole muscle shut, as it had shut on a green rock in the dark, and held him where he stood with a goose looking at him. It let go when he had counted twenty, and he went on, and it gave him eleven more.
     6	
     7	It was forty-four strides from the barn to the well, so that the yard came to four elevens exactly, and he was more pleased by that than the fact deserved.
     8	
     9	He did with it what he did with everything, which was to measure the thing and then live inside the measure. He drew water in elevens, and crossed to the long house in three of them and a short one; and by noon he had learned to stop at the tenth stride of his own accord, in some place where a person might reasonably be standing, so that it should look like a choice.
