   210	## Continuity Rules
   211	
   212	1. **The Compact Registry is the authority on Path classification.** Any scene involving official rank must be consistent with what the Registry would show for that character at that point in the story.
   213	
   214	2. **Path declarations follow standard format.** Only Cael's fragment notices deviate. All other practitioners receive standard declarations.
   215	
   216	3. **Tier advancement is not instant.** No character advances a tier in a single scene. Advancement is earned across multiple books for major characters.
   217	
   218	4. **The Architect's will is not omniscient.** It can detect [SHATTERED] signatures and issue systemic pressure, but it cannot directly perceive or target individuals until Book 11 when it becomes actively deployed.
   219	
   220	5. **The Quieting spreads from ancient sites outward.** It does not appear suddenly in cities. It begins at edge-territory ruins and expands. This gives the companions time to investigate before it becomes a continental crisis.
   221	
   222	6. **Cael's ability integration has limits.** He cannot integrate an ability he has not witnessed in use. Seeing a written description does not qualify. The ability must be performed in his presence.
   223	
   224	7. **The found family is permanent.** No companion exits the series without narrative justification. The loss in Book 12 is a choice, not a death — the companion is still alive, their relationship with Cael changed.
   225	
     1	# THE FRACTURED PATH — SERIES BIBLE v0.1
     2	
     3	**Canon status: LOCKED (arc structure); PROVISIONAL (per-book details)**
     4	**15 books, one protagonist, continuous series**
     5	**Protagonist:** Cael (Caelen) — ages 14 to ~27 across the series (**LOCKED to the drafted timeline, 2026-09-04:** fourteen at Book 1; eighteen on the page at Book 6 Ch14; nineteen on the page at Book 7 Ch14; one birthday per autumn thereafter (a book that does not cross an autumn stages no birthday — Book 8) — the arc bands below are rebased to this and prose is never retrofitted to the old bands)
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## Series Identity
    10	
    11	A young man's Kindling at age 14 returns a classification no living person has seen: [SHATTERED]. In Valdris, where your Path determines your rank, your access, and your right to remain inside city walls, [SHATTERED] means expulsion. The last time it appeared, eleven people died.
    12	
    13	Over fifteen books Cael discovers that [SHATTERED] is not a broken Path. It is what Paths were before someone decided they needed to be organized, capped, and controlled.
    14	
    15	**The series asks:** When the world builds a system to contain something it fears, and the thing it fears turns out to be *you* — what do you do with that?
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	## Tone
    20	
    21	He Who Fights Monsters × Iron Prince × R.A. Salvatore.
    22	
    23	- **HWFM register:** dry humor, protagonist self-awareness, willingness to find the absurdity in terrible situations
    24	- **Iron Prince register:** institutional antagonism, earned underdog progression, companions who matter as much as the protagonist
    25	- **Salvatore register:** vivid physical combat with specific choreography, found family as the emotional core, identity arc that runs the full series
    26	
    27	**Clean and shareable.** No explicit content. Mild language calibrated to PG-13. Violence is present and consequential but not gratuitous. The emotional stakes are always human.
    28	
    29	---
    30	
    31	## The Promise by Arc
    32	
    33	- **Arc 1:** Make the reader want to stay in the Unranked District with Cael and Lira.
    34	- **Arc 2:** Make the reader want to be at the academy.
    35	- **Arc 3:** Make the reader feel the edge territories as the first place Cael has ever been fully free.
    36	- **Arc 4:** Make the reader feel the weight of what it costs to be the only person who can stop this.
    37	- **Arc 5:** Make the reader feel that the ending was earned by every single chapter that came before it.
    38	
    39	---
    40	
    41	## Protagonist Arc (full series)
    42	
    43	Cael moves from *I will survive what the world did to me* → *I will understand what I actually am* → *I will decide what to do with it.*
    44	
    45	The identity arc is Salvatore's Drizzt model: the world decided what Cael was before he could. Every book is a fight to replace that definition with his own — not through denial, not through rage, but through accumulated proof of who he actually is.
    46	
    47	The found family arc is the emotional spine. Cael does not become powerful and then earn companions. He earns companions first, and their faith in him is what makes the power coherent.
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	## Planned Scale
    52	
    53	- ~110,000 words per book
    54	- 24 chapters per book (~4,600 words per chapter)
    55	- Total series target: ~1,650,000 words
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	# ARC 1 — THE FRACTURE
    60	**Books 1–3 | Ages 14–16 (drafted) | Antagonist: Warden Coss and the machinery of expulsion**
    61	
    62	The arc that establishes everything: who Cael is, what the world thinks of him, and why he chooses to fight rather than disappear.
    63	
    64	---
    65	
    66	## Book 1 — The Shattered
    67	
    68	**Promise:** Make the reader feel expelled alongside Cael — and then make them want to stay.
    69	
    70	**External arc:** Cael's Kindling returns [SHATTERED]. He is expelled from Denvash within 24 hours. He reaches Ardenmere, a major city with a large Unranked District, and has to figure out how to survive without a Path, a rank, or legal standing.
    71	
    72	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *the world has decided I'm dangerous* to *fine, then I'm going to be dangerous on my terms.*
    73	
    74	**Companion:** Lira joins. Wind Path, Copper-tier, previously expelled from an academy for insubordination. She picks Cael as a companion because outcasts are more interesting than ranked students. Their dynamic starts as mutual survival and becomes something else by the end.
    75	
    76	**First power revelation:** Cael realizes he can access shards of the abilities he's observed — imperfectly, partially, without any fragment notice to explain it. He uses a version of Lira's Wind ability in a fight without understanding how. **As drafted (Book 1, Ch16 — ratified revision):** he tells her directly, once he's had two weeks to sit with it privately first — a stronger beat than the original "he doesn't tell her" plan, since it deepens the relationship arc (disclosure → trust → the Ch21 conflict-and-repair beat) rather than banking a secret for a later reveal. Lira does not flinch; she'd already half-guessed. The dialogue between them stays appropriately hedged about the *mechanism* (how it works, why her specifically) — that ambiguity, not the fact of the borrowing itself, is what Book 3 pays off. Book 2 and onward should treat Lira as a knowing, willing participant in Cael's fragment development, not someone being kept in the dark.
    77	
    78	**Key scene:** Cael's Kindling. The Arbiter appears, goes silent for 11 seconds (long enough that the certification officer starts to sweat), and resolves to [SHATTERED]. The officer's face. The room clearing. Hesk walking Cael out through the back exit.
    79	
    80	**Ending:** Cael wins his first real public fight — an unsanctioned circuit match that he wasn't supposed to be allowed to enter — and the Guilds Compact receives an automatic flag from the Arbiter system that nobody in the Compact knows how to interpret. Someone decides to find out who this is.
    81	
    82	**Mystery introduced:** Why does [SHATTERED] produce a Compact flag? Nobody on the current staff knows.
    83	
    84	---
    85	
    86	## Book 2 — Iron Circuit
    87	
    88	**Promise:** Make the reader feel the underground circuit as a legitimate world — brutal, fair in its own way, and the only place Cael can actually compete.
    89	
    90	**External arc:** Cael and Lira operate in Ardenmere's unsanctioned ranked circuit — underground fights that accept any practitioner regardless of classification. The circuit has its own hierarchy, its own rules, and its own politics. Cael is an anomaly in a space built for anomalies.
    91	
    92	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I survived the expulsion* to *I might actually be good at this.*
    93	
    94	**Companion:** Brom joins. Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier. From a wealthy family who expected Gold from him; Copper Iron Skin was a humiliation in their eyes. He left before they could push him out. He finds Cael in the circuit because a practitioner who can't be classified fighting people two tiers above their rating is someone worth watching. He is the first person who sees Cael's power as interesting rather than frightening.
    95	
    96	**Power development:** The first fragment notice appears — incomplete, unnamed, but real. Cael begins keeping a private log of what he can do and what he can't.
    97	
    98	**Antagonist escalation:** The Guilds Compact's junior monitoring office identifies Cael as the [SHATTERED] flag origin. They don't know what to do about him yet. They begin passive surveillance.
    99	
   100	**Key scene:** Cael and Brom's first fight — against each other, in the circuit, before they're companions. Brom wins. After, he sits down next to Cael and says: *I want to know how you did that thing in the third exchange.* This is how their friendship begins.
   101	
   102	**Ending:** Cael wins a high-profile circuit match against an Iron-tier opponent. The match is witnessed by a scout from a minor academy. An offer arrives — unofficial, technically not legal — to attend under a workaround classification. Cael accepts.
   103	
   104	---
   105	
   106	## Book 3 — No Path Given
   107	
   108	**Promise:** Make the reader want to be at this academy — and dread what happens when the institution finds out what Cael is.
   109	
   110	**External arc:** Cael attends the Greyvane Academy under a workaround classification. The academy is minor-tier but legitimate. He is technically enrolled as an unclassified observer — a category that exists for children who haven't Kindled yet, which he absolutely is not. The Compact's monitoring escalates when they track him to an official academy.
   111	
   112	**Internal arc:** Cael moves from *I can do this* to *I need to understand what I'm actually doing before it gets someone killed.*
   113	
   114	**Companion:** Karis joins. Ember Path, Iron-tier, was top-ranked at a much better academy. She transferred to Greyvane specifically because she heard about the [SHATTERED] practitioner and wants to understand how the Fractured Path works. She is the first person who studies Cael's power as a phenomenon rather than a threat or an asset.
   115	
   116	**First real revelation:** Cael, in a training match against Karis, absorbs a fragment of her Ember ability mid-fight — consciously, intentionally, for the first time. He receives a fragment notice immediately after. The integration is partial but real. He understands for the first time that what he does is not random.
   117	
   118	**Antagonist:** Warden Coss reappears — now revealed to be a Compact agent rather than just a local official. He has been tracking Cael since Denvash. He brings a formal challenge to Cael's enrollment, which forces a public adjudication hearing at the academy.
   119	
   120	**Key scene:** The adjudication hearing. Cael cannot prove his enrollment is legitimate — because it isn't. He wins the hearing by demonstrating that [SHATTERED] is not a classification the Compact's rules actually cover, which means their authority to expel him from the academy is technically contested. It is the most technical argument a fifteen-year-old has ever made in an official hearing, and it works because he spent three weeks studying Compact registry law.
   121	
   122	**Ending:** Cael wins the hearing and keeps his enrollment. He also makes a public record of his win — which means every Compact official above Coss's level now knows his name. The monitoring becomes active tracking. Somewhere, a file is opened.
   123	
   124	**Mystery planted:** Karis finds a reference to [SHATTERED] in Greyvane's old records — pre-Compact, from before the registry standardization. The word used is different: [UNBOUND]. She copies the passage before the record is confiscated.
   125	
