   246	
   247	Seln took the cold road on the thirtieth morning, and that was the last of the five.
   248	
   249	He had walked the east road on the ninth evening, within an hour of stepping off a ferry, as far as a saddle and a little beyond it, and the north before light on the tenth. The south road had cost him two mornings, since it forked. The fourth was the road they had all come in by, and he had gone back along that one too, three miles, to see it as a person sees it who is arriving. This was the fifth. It left the north road at a dead tree and climbed, and the meadow called it cold; he learned why at the first turn, where a wind came down off bare ground that had no business in such mild weather.
   250	
   251	He went up it in the grey, alone, at the pace he had once taught four young people in an orchard.
   252	
   253	Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
   254	
   255	It had not gone. He walked, and at each turn of the track he did the three things that he had always done: he found the place where a man would stand who wished to watch the road without being seen from it, and the place where the road was narrowest, and he looked for water, and paced it from where a camp might lie.
   256	
   257	Then, since he could not help it, he wrote it up.
