editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:229:It was the middle of the morning on the sixtieth day. They had crossed the wash at first light, with Brom walking wide of the cart and both hands behind his back, and they had come over the saddle slowly. Pike was not at his plank, and the book was not out; a boy at the nearest fire said that he was down at the landing, since the ferry had brought a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:He had always taken an inventory at the start of anything. For two years it had been the same one, a count of the fragments he carried and how each of them lay idle; and on the fifty-fourth morning he caught himself at the head of a ladder, taking another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:193:The carter was back on the fifty-seventh day, an hour after noon, as he had been before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:265:So on the fifty-eighth morning she sat on the tail-board of a cart in Thornwater's yard with the calfskin unrolled beside her. She could not hold its corners down, and so Brom had brought four stones from the well's foot, and set one on each, and stepped back with his arm in its sling, looking as pleased as if he had been allowed to carry something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:They came to it in the morning of the fifty-second day, with the sun still behind the eastern hill and the grass grey with dew. The ruts ran down through a gap in the scrub onto a beach of pale stones, and went into the water, and the carter stopped his mule ten yards short and stood with his fist in the cheek-strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:133:"He came down the cold road on the fifty-seventh, with his three and his mules, and he walked to the plank before he'd had so much as a wash, and stood in front of the place where that slip had been." He turned the cup. "Then he asked me for it. I'd kept it by, struck, as I'd struck it, and I gave it him, and he read it twice; I counted, since it's what I'd have done. Then he had me open the book at your leaf, and he read your line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:163:Seln was gone before light on the sixty-first day. He came back at dusk, and sat down in his own place, a little behind the rest, and held out his hands to the fire. There was pale dust on his boots to the ankle, the dust of the south road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:167:They went out on the sixty-second, slowly, since it was a crew with three good legs. The south road ran level through river meadows, and the leg that had given eleven strides at Thornwater gave twenty now and wanted a shorter rest. Lira would not ride anything; she went the whole way on the ash stick with her mouth shut, and at noon they came to the fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:339:Pike said the well on the sixty-fourth morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:361:She ran on the sixty-fifth, at first light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:403:Brom took the cloth off his arm on the sixty-sixth, at the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:423:Karis held a pencil on the sixty-eighth day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:435:He wrote the long entry on the sixty-ninth evening, at the boards by the inn door, where the light was. It was the hour at which he had once written that he was free, and he had not gone back to read that page, since there was no need.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:443:*Mine: the calf is a leg on level ground and gives nine strides uphill. Lira: no stick since the sixty-fourth. She runs. The ankle bends less, and later; those are her words, and I have not counted since the first run. Brom: shut, not sound, and wrapped. He has carried nothing for sixteen days and I think it is the hardest thing he has done past the Line. Karis: a pencil, yesterday. Seln: nothing to report, which he reported.*

