editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:49:Somebody of his had been here first. He was eighteen years old, and in all that time nobody had told him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:1:# Chapter 13 — Nobody's Measuring
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:373:"It bends less, and later," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:443:*Mine: the calf is a leg on level ground and gives nine strides uphill. Lira: no stick since the sixty-fourth. She runs. The ankle bends less, and later; those are her words, and I have not counted since the first run. Brom: shut, not sound, and wrapped. He has carried nothing for sixteen days and I think it is the hardest thing he has done past the Line. Karis: a pencil, yesterday. Seln: nothing to report, which he reported.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:167:"No. I'll put it properly. That's everything I know about the wrong country." Her chin went along the shelves, left to right. "Every leaf I've copied since I could hold a pen, and every one of them is about the place we walked out of: tiers, and ranks, and what an Arbiter station does with a person. I've read forty accounts of how a Path is assessed, and there isn't a page in three boxes that has the word *stillhound* on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:307:"You mean the quiet ground?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:357:"Not a good one. The first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:"They stop inside, where the tiers are," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:341:"Held a dog with a shoulder and a river, that one," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:235:"It's holding the foot the right way round," she said. "It comes off when there's a roof and a lamp, and not before. I've said I'm fine. I'd be obliged if everybody would go on believing it till then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:261:"I've stood over that valley thirty years," he said, in his dry unused voice. "I know what it sounds like when they take a cart. You can hear it from the fence, with the wind right." He turned his eyes on Lira. "I stood on that bank through the whole of this, and none of you shouted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:293:"The nearest walks a route and she's not due." The woman wrung out a cloth. "Boot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:329:"Four," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:333:"Out of five. Two men went down to that ford this month and one came up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:157:He was still large.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:283:"I'm fine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:63:"Dusk. Never noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:79:"River's their colour," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:109:"It's because you die," said the steading's woman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:213:"*Not accounted for: 110 yards. The hour.*" She read the last line more quietly. "*Evidence against: none, which I don't trust.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:257:"Never two at once," said Karis, writing, "never the same person twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:43:"Ford's had a third."

