editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:179:It was a feinter. She had met a dozen of them, at Fenmark, on a chalked floor, with a coach walking the edge who gave a mark for every feint that was answered in time; and she had been the best in her year at it, since her answer was always the same one. When something came at your knee you were simply somewhere else. You were there before the eye of the girl opposite had finished telling her that you had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:Her whole body knew what to do with this animal. She was Wind. The burst sat in her calves like a held breath, and it had sat there since she was fourteen, and for fifty days she had been taking a stride at a time away from it, on a river bar, with a boy telling her how who had been her partner since a lane in Ardenmere. *You say it at the first stride. Then you say it again at the second.* She said it at the snap. She said it again as the thing sprang back, and again as it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:265:Her right foot rolled outward, and went on rolling. It went past the place where an ankle stops, and she heard it go. It made a small wet sound, like a green twig bent double, and she knew that sound. She had heard it once at Fenmark from a girl two places above her on the list, who had not been on the list the term after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He had gone to a good many fights. He had gone to them down a lane in Ardenmere, and up the steps of a hall at Norhold, and across sixty feet of sprung oak every week for two years; and in every case somebody else had drawn the card. A keeper had matched him, or a bracket had, or a house that needed its charter, and he had walked out onto whatever floor they pointed to and tried to lose as little as he could. Nobody had drawn this one. A slip had been laid on a plank for anyone at all, and he had put up his hand, and reached for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:31:She had her shoulder to the gate already. Lira had been watching her from the cart's tail with a frown, and Cael knew that frown, for it had been turned on a sheet of sailcloth by a hearth, and on a card at Norhold, and on every list she had ever stood under.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:173:"At Norhold I lost to a girl that a whole program had built," said Lira. "You were all at the rail. When it was over there was a sheet, with figures on it, and it told me to a tenth of a point how far I was from Silver. I hated that sheet, and I kept it; I've still got it somewhere, in with the certificate." She looked into the flames. "Then on the hill they made a ladder to my height. Nobody had ever made me anything. They built it, and put my name on the bottom rung, and there was a rung for every week after. I walked off it. I paid the whole price for walking off it, and I'd do it again tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:159:He knew most of what was in them. There were the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper, and the registry's printed sheet of the continent, still rolled; there were her lecture notes from Ternhall, and the passages she had copied in a restricted room, a line at a time, before her standing lapsed. All of it had come over a ridge by hand, and down a scree, and across fifty days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:239:He had had a great many written about him. A panel had given him one for a bout, and it had been arranged beforehand; a house had kept a rate for him that was scattered on purpose, so that it should never settle into a line that anybody could read. The registry had never managed a tier or a rank for him, and had settled on a word. There had been a question mark on a board in Ardenmere, and a level on an advisory that he had never been shown. Some of those figures had flattered him and some had been meant to sink him, and he could not think of one that had simply been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:117:"You'll land locked. With that thing six feet off." She had a hand round her own shin, above the boot, and the knuckles were white. "You've a rule about that. You've had it since Ardenmere. You made me learn it."

